# Source for q/spi-optimize3.js: SPI Optimize images third batch (Instrumentation + Resolution).
I="Instrumentation"; R="Resolution"
Q=[
# --- Beam forming ---
(I,"How does a phased array point the beam in different directions?","Firing the elements with small time delays in a sloped pattern",["Moving the crystal with a motor","Firing all elements at exactly the same time","Changing the frequency"],"The delay pattern sets the beam direction.","F"),
(I,"A phased array focuses the beam by:","Using a curved pattern of firing delays, with the outer elements fired first",["Using only the center element","Moving the lens","Turning off the outer elements"],"Changing the curve moves the focal depth.","F"),
(I,"Which statement describes dynamic receive focusing?","The system changes receive delays continuously so echoes from every depth are focused",["The transmit focus moves automatically","Only one depth is focused","No focusing on receive"],"It improves lateral resolution along the whole image without lowering frame rate.","C"),
(I,"Apodization is:","Using different voltages on the elements, stronger in the center and weaker at the edges",["Turning off the machine","Changing frequency","Moving the probe"],"It reduces side lobes and grating lobes.","C"),
(I,"Grating lobes are more likely when:","Element spacing is too wide compared with the wavelength",["Elements are very close together","Apodization is used","Subdicing is used"],"Subdicing (cutting elements smaller) reduces grating lobes.","C"),
(I,"A 1.5-D array improves:","Elevational resolution (slice thickness), by focusing in the elevation plane",["Only axial resolution","Only temporal resolution","Doppler sensitivity only"],"Standard 1-D arrays use a fixed lens for elevation focus.","C"),
(I,"An annular array transducer focuses:","In all planes (lateral and elevation) at once, because the elements are rings",["Only in one plane","Not at all","Only in the near field"],"It must be steered mechanically.","C"),
(I,"The acoustic lens on a linear or phased array mainly sets:","Fixed elevation focus",["Lateral steering","Transmit frequency","Pulse length"],"Elevation focus cannot be changed by the user on standard arrays.","F"),
# --- Harmonics ---
(I,"With a fundamental frequency of 2 MHz, the second harmonic is:","4 MHz",["1 MHz","2 MHz","6 MHz"],"Harmonics are whole-number multiples of the fundamental.","F"),
(I,"Tissue harmonics are created because:","Sound travels slightly faster in compressions than rarefactions, so the wave distorts",["The transducer sends harmonics directly","Air creates them","Reflections double the frequency"],"This nonlinear behavior builds up with depth.","F"),
(I,"Harmonic signals are weak in the near field, which helps because:","Near-field clutter and reverberation are reduced",["The near field becomes brighter","Penetration is lost","Frame rate doubles"],"Harmonics build up as the wave travels.","F"),
(I,"Harmonics form mostly in the strongest central part of the beam, which:","Reduces side-lobe artifacts and improves lateral resolution",["Widens the beam","Increases grating lobes","Lowers contrast"],"Weak side-lobe energy produces few harmonics.","F"),
(I,"How does pulse inversion separate harmonic echoes from fundamental echoes?","Sending two inverted pulses and adding the echoes, so fundamentals cancel and harmonics remain",["Sending one long pulse","Filtering out all echoes","Using CW"],"It keeps a broad bandwidth, preserving axial resolution.","C"),
(I,"For harmonic imaging, the transducer needs:","A wide bandwidth to receive at twice the transmit frequency",["A narrow bandwidth","No damping","A lower frequency only"],"The receive filter is set around the harmonic frequency.","F"),
# --- Frame rate math ---
(R,"At 10 cm depth, each scan line takes about:","130 µs",["13 µs","1.3 ms","13 ms"],"13 µs per cm of depth for the round trip.","F"),
(R,"At 10 cm depth with 100 lines per frame, the time for one frame is about:","13 ms, giving about 77 frames per second",["130 ms, giving about 7 fps","1.3 ms, giving about 770 fps","1 second"],"100 × 130 µs = 13 ms.","C"),
(R,"At 20 cm depth with 200 lines and one focus, the maximum frame rate is about:","19 frames per second",["77 frames per second","385 frames per second","3 frames per second"],"77,000 ÷ (20 × 200) ≈ 19 fps.","C"),
(R,"A sector of 90° is narrowed to 45° with the same line density. Frame rate:","About doubles",["Halves","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"Half the lines are needed for each frame.","F"),
(R,"Using three transmit focal zones instead of one, with all else the same, makes the frame rate:","About one-third as fast",["Three times faster","The same","Twice as fast"],"Each focal zone needs its own pulse on every line.","F"),
(R,"Multi-line acquisition with 4 receive lines per transmit can raise frame rate by up to about:","4 times",["2 times","10 times","No change"],"It is widely used in 3D and cardiac imaging.","C"),
# --- Digital and display ---
(I,"How many bits per pixel are needed for 64 shades of gray?","6",["4","8","64"],"2⁶ = 64.","F"),
(I,"A system with 4 bits per pixel can display how many shades of gray?","16",["4","8","256"],"2⁴ = 16.","F"),
(I,"How many bits are needed to show 1,024 shades of gray?","10",["8","12","32"],"2¹⁰ = 1,024.","C"),
(I,"The binary number 101 equals the decimal number:","5",["3","101","6"],"1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 5.","F"),
(I,"The scan converter in an ultrasound system:","Stores the image in memory and converts scan lines into a display format",["Creates the pulse","Amplifies the echoes","Cleans the probe"],"Modern systems use digital scan converters.","F"),
(I,"Fill-in interpolation is:","Estimating missing pixel values between scan lines",["Removing artifacts","Adding gain","Raising frame rate"],"It makes sector images look smooth deep in the image where lines spread apart.","C"),
(I,"Lossy image compression (such as JPEG) differs from lossless compression because it:","Permanently removes some image data to reduce file size",["Keeps every detail","Makes files larger","Is not used in medicine"],"Lossless compression keeps all original data.","F"),
(I,"A display matrix with more pixels (for example 1,024 × 1,024 instead of 512 × 512) improves:","Spatial resolution of the displayed image",["Temporal resolution","Penetration","Axial resolution of the beam"],"More, smaller pixels show finer detail.","F"),
(I,"The dynamic range of echoes coming back from the body is about:","100 dB or more, which must be compressed to fit the display",["5 dB","1 dB","10,000 dB"],"Displays and the eye handle a much smaller range.","C"),
(I,"Increasing receiver gain by 3 dB:","Doubles the signal power on the display",["Halves the signal","Has no effect","Multiplies it by 10"],"10 dB multiplies power by 10.","F"),
(I,"Raising receiver gain changes:","Image brightness only, not patient exposure",["Patient exposure","Frequency","Wavelength"],"Output power changes both brightness and exposure.","F"),
(I,"If the image is dark and receiver gain is already at maximum, the next step is to:","Increase output power, keeping ALARA in mind",["Lower the gain","Turn off TGC","Increase depth"],"First try gain, TGC, frequency and focus.","F"),
# --- Pre and post processing ---
(I,"Which is an example of pre-processing?","Persistence (frame averaging)",["Read zoom","Changing a frozen image's gray map","Measuring with calipers"],"Pre-processing happens before images are stored.","F"),
(I,"Which can be changed after the image is frozen?","Gray map, B-color and read zoom",["TGC","Output power","Frequency"],"Post-processing changes how stored data is displayed.","F"),
(I,"A drawback of spatial compounding is that it can:","Hide useful shadowing and enhancement and blur moving structures",["Increase speckle","Raise frame rate","Make edges rough"],"It averages images from several angles.","F"),
(I,"A drawback of speckle reduction filtering is:","Loss of very fine detail if set too high",["More speckle","Lower penetration","Higher exposure"],"Use moderate levels for cardiac imaging.","F"),
(I,"A one-touch 'auto optimize' button usually adjusts:","Gain and TGC across the image",["Frequency and output power only","Doppler angle","Transducer type"],"Check the result, since auto settings may not suit every patient.","F"),
(I,"Horizontal bands of brightness at certain depths after adding several focal zones may be:","Focal zone banding, from joining zones together",["A real structure","Aliasing","Mirror image"],"Adjust TGC or focal zone placement.","C"),
(I,"Lateral gain compensation adjusts brightness:","Across the image from side to side",["By depth only","Only in Doppler","Only in M-mode"],"It helps when one side of a sector is dimmer.","F"),
(I,"A good order for optimizing a new 2D image is:","Preset, depth, focus, gain and TGC, then frequency, harmonics and dynamic range",["Output power first, then nothing else","Measure first, then adjust","Only change the gray map"],"Adjust the basics before fine-tuning.","F"),
(I,"For an obese patient with a poor far field, a reasonable combination is:","Lower frequency, harmonics, a deeper focus and higher TGC in the far field",["Higher frequency and shallow focus","Turn off harmonics","Reduce depth so the heart is cut off"],"Raise output only if needed, following ALARA.","F"),
(I,"A cine loop is useful because it:","Lets the sonographer review recent frames after freezing",["Increases frame rate","Changes the frequency","Increases output power"],"It helps catch the best frame for measurements.","F"),
(I,"An LCD monitor that is too bright can cause:","Incorrect gain settings because the image looks brighter than it really is",["No problems","Better diagnosis","Higher frame rate"],"Set the monitor in a dimly lit room before adjusting gain.","F"),
# --- Resolution concepts ---
(R,"Range resolution is another name for:","Axial resolution",["Lateral resolution","Elevational resolution","Temporal resolution"],"It depends on spatial pulse length.","F"),
(R,"Slice thickness resolution is another name for:","Elevational resolution",["Axial resolution","Temporal resolution","Contrast resolution"],"Thick slices can fill small cystic spaces with false echoes.","F"),
(R,"A pulse with a frequency of 3 MHz and 2 cycles in soft tissue has a spatial pulse length of about:","1.0 mm",["0.5 mm","2.0 mm","3.0 mm"],"λ = 1.54 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.51 mm; SPL = 2 × 0.51 ≈ 1.03 mm.","C"),
(R,"The axial resolution of the pulse above (3 MHz, 2 cycles) is about:","0.5 mm",["1.0 mm","2.0 mm","0.25 mm"],"Axial resolution = SPL ÷ 2.","C"),
(R,"Increasing frequency from 2 MHz to 4 MHz, with the same number of cycles, makes axial resolution:","About twice as good (the number halves)",["Twice as bad","The same","Four times worse"],"Shorter wavelength gives shorter pulses.","F"),
(R,"Lateral resolution at the focus is about:","Half the transducer diameter (for a flat, unfocused disc)",["Twice the diameter","Equal to the pulse length","Equal to the frequency"],"Beam width at the end of the near zone is half the diameter.","C"),
(R,"Increasing transducer diameter (with frequency the same) affects the far field by:","Reducing divergence and improving lateral resolution deep in the image",["Increasing divergence","Worsening lateral resolution everywhere","Having no effect"],"A larger aperture also lengthens the near zone.","C"),
(R,"Which statement about axial and lateral resolution is true?","Axial resolution is usually better (a smaller number) than lateral resolution",["Lateral is always better","They are always equal","Neither affects image quality"],"Pulses are shorter than beams are wide.","F"),
(R,"Which change helps the display show subtle differences in tissue brightness?","More bits per pixel and appropriate dynamic range and compression",["Fewer bits per pixel","Lower frame rate only","Turning off TGC"],"More gray shades show subtle tissue differences.","F"),
(R,"Temporal resolution is most important when imaging:","Fast-moving valves and heart walls",["Liver","Thyroid","Bone"],"Echo needs high frame rates.","F"),
(R,"Which set of changes gives the best temporal resolution?","Shallow depth, narrow sector, single focus and low line density",["Deep depth, wide sector, multiple foci","Wide sector and high line density","Many focal zones"],"Each choice reduces time per frame.","F"),
(R,"Lowering line density to raise frame rate will worsen:","Lateral resolution (spatial resolution)",["Axial resolution","Penetration","Doppler sensitivity"],"Fewer lines leave larger gaps between scan lines.","F"),
(R,"Improving elevational resolution reduces which artifact?","Slice-thickness artifact (false echoes in fluid-filled spaces)",["Aliasing","Mirror image","Acoustic shadowing"],"1.5-D arrays and lenses narrow the slice.","F"),
(R,"Why does a higher frequency transducer improve both axial and lateral resolution?","Shorter wavelength shortens pulses, and higher frequency diverges less in the far field",["It uses more power","It reduces attenuation","It creates harmonics only"],"The trade-off is less penetration.","F"),
(R,"Speckle is best described as:","A grainy interference pattern, not a true image of tissue structure",["A true image of each cell","A Doppler signal","Noise from electrical interference"],"Speckle tracking uses this pattern to measure motion.","F"),
(R,"Which control improves spatial resolution in a region without changing the transducer?","Write zoom (with more scan lines in the box)",["Read zoom","Gray map","B-color"],"Read zoom only enlarges existing pixels.","F"),
]
